{
  "image_name": "7e8b35d1132c488f0724662f7bbb1bcf.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how two kinds of marine food production—wild capture fisheries and aquaculture—alter ocean biogeochemistry through physical disturbance, material flows, and food-web change.\n\nIn the upper half, capture fisheries are shown affecting coastal and offshore waters through fuel use and chemical discharge, the spread of non-native species by ships, and lost fishing gear that creates habitat while also trapping animals. Fishing removes biomass and therefore elements from the sea, while overfishing at one trophic level appears to reorganize the food web. Smaller fish populations are linked to reduced fecal export and vertical migration. Bottom trawling is highlighted as a major seabed disturbance: it exposes deeper sediments to oxygen, resuspends and relocates sediments, and disrupts benthic organisms. Added structures on the seafloor also replace natural habitat and provide hard substrate for other organisms.\n\nThe lower half shows aquaculture producing a different but related set of effects. Coastal pond construction removes habitat and may increase greenhouse-gas release and groundwater discharge. Farming adds feed-derived nutrients and organic matter, while harvest removes biomass. Non-fed aquaculture filters suspended particles, fish pens alter local currents, and waste accumulation on the seafloor changes oxygen conditions, nutrient cycling, gas fluxes, and benthic communities. Seaweed farming is depicted as taking up carbon and nutrients, and particulate matter is exported offshore.",
